To answer your original question, I'll provide some examples of how you can use Jsoup to parse HTML and create elements:

1. **Parsing HTML:**

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.select.Elements;

public class ParseHtml {
    @org.junit.Test
    public void testParseHtml() {
        String html = "<html><body><div>Example Div</div></body></html>";
        Document document = Jsoup.connect("http://example.com").get();
        Elements divs = document.select("div");
        for (org.jsoup.nodes.Element div : divs) {
            System.out.println(div.text());
        }
    }
}
```

2. **Creating HTML elements:**

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;

public class CreateHtmlElements {
    @org.junit.Test
    public void testCreateElement() {
        String html = "<html><body></body></html>";
        Document document = Jsoup.parse(html);
        Element div = document.createElement("div");
        div.appendText("Example Div");
        System.out.println(div.toString());
    }
}
```

3. **Creating HTML elements with attributes:**

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Element;

public class CreateHtmlElementsWithAttributes {
    @org.junit.Test
    public void testCreateElementWithAttribute() {
        String html = "<html><body></body></html>";
        Document document = Jsoup.parse(html);
        Element span = document.createElement("span");
        span.attr("class", "example-class");
        span.appendText("Example Span");
        System.out.println(span.toString());
    }
}
```

These examples demonstrate how you can use JSoup to parse HTML, create elements, and add attributes to those elements.
